# Math & Science Foundations — everyday words used across AP math and science courses.
# AP Lexicon additions; original definitions pending teacher review.
@subject foundations-math-science | Math & Science Foundations | 数理基础词汇 | Foundations | cyan | 16
@desc Everyday English words that AP math and science questions assume you already know: shapes, numbers, graphs, lab work and basic science.
@framework Not an AP course. A cross-course foundation set; words appear throughout AP math and science exams.

@unit 1 | Geometry and Measurement | 几何与测量
@topic 1.1 | Circles and Angles | 圆与角 | Use circle and angle vocabulary in word problems
- radius | 半径 | The distance from the center of a circle or sphere to any point on its edge | 圆心到圆上任意一点的距离，是直径的一半。复数为 radii。 | A wheel with a radius of 0.3 m travels 2π(0.3) m per turn. | 1
  mis: The radius is the full width of the circle — that is the diameter; the radius is half of it.
  vs: diameter
  rel: circumference; area
- diameter | 直径 | A straight line through the center of a circle from one side to the other; twice the radius | 经过圆心、两端都在圆上的线段，长度是半径的两倍。 | The pipe's diameter is 4 cm, so its radius is 2 cm. | 1
  vs: radius
- circumference | 周长（圆周长） | The distance around a circle, C = 2πr | 圆一周的长度，C = 2πr = πd。 | One revolution of the tire covers a distance equal to its circumference. | 1
  vs: area
- arc | 弧 | A part of the circumference of a circle | 圆周上的一段。弧长 = rθ（θ 为弧度）。 | An object moving through an arc of 90° travels one quarter of the circle. | 2
- chord | 弦 | A line segment joining two points on a circle | 连接圆上两点的线段，直径是最长的弦。 | The longest chord of a circle is its diameter. | 2
- sector | 扇形 | The region of a circle between two radii and an arc | 由两条半径和一段弧围成的区域。 | A pie chart divides a circle into sectors. | 2
- angle | 角 | The amount of turn between two lines that meet at a point | 两条射线在一点相交所形成的图形，用度或弧度表示大小。 | The ramp makes a 30° angle with the ground. | 1
- right angle | 直角 | An angle of exactly 90° | 恰好 90° 的角。 | The normal force acts at a right angle to the surface. | 1
- acute angle | 锐角 | An angle less than 90° | 小于 90° 的角。 | A projectile launched at an acute angle rises and moves forward. | 1
  vs: obtuse angle
- obtuse angle | 钝角 | An angle greater than 90° but less than 180° | 大于 90° 小于 180° 的角。 | The two forces meet at an obtuse angle of 120°. | 1
  vs: acute angle
- complementary angles | 互余角 | Two angles that add up to 90° | 和为 90° 的两个角。 | On an incline, the angles θ and 90° − θ are complementary angles. | 2
  vs: supplementary angles
- supplementary angles | 互补角 | Two angles that add up to 180° | 和为 180° 的两个角。 | Angles on a straight line are supplementary angles. | 2
  vs: complementary angles
@topic 1.2 | Shapes and Solids | 平面图形与立体图形 | Name common shapes and solids
- polygon | 多边形 | A closed flat shape made of straight sides | 由三条或更多直线段首尾相连围成的平面图形。 | A hexagon is a polygon with six sides. | 1
- quadrilateral | 四边形 | A polygon with four sides | 有四条边的多边形，如正方形、长方形、梯形。 | Rectangles, rhombuses and trapezoids are all quadrilaterals. | 1
- parallelogram | 平行四边形 | A quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides | 两组对边分别平行的四边形。 | Adding two vectors head-to-tail forms a parallelogram. | 2
- trapezoid | 梯形 | A quadrilateral with exactly one pair of parallel sides | 只有一组对边平行的四边形（美式说法）。 | The trapezoidal rule estimates area using trapezoids. | 2
- rectangle | 长方形（矩形） | A quadrilateral with four right angles | 四个角都是直角的四边形。 | The area under a constant velocity graph is a rectangle. | 1
- sphere | 球体 | A perfectly round three-dimensional object; every surface point is the same distance from the center | 所有表面点到球心距离相等的立体。体积 V = 4/3πr³。 | Gauss's law uses a sphere around a point charge. | 1
- cylinder | 圆柱体 | A solid with two parallel circular bases joined by a curved surface | 两个平行圆形底面和一个曲面组成的立体，V = πr²h。 | A graduated cylinder measures liquid volume. | 1
- cone | 圆锥体 | A solid with a circular base that narrows to a point | 一个圆形底面收窄到一个顶点的立体，V = 1/3πr²h。 | Water drains from a cone-shaped tank in a related-rates problem. | 2
- cube | 立方体（正方体） | A solid with six equal square faces | 六个面都是相同正方形的立体。 | A cube with side 2 cm has a volume of 8 cm³. | 1
- prism | 棱柱 | A solid with two identical parallel polygon bases and flat sides | 两个全等且平行的多边形底面、侧面为平行四边形的立体。 | Light passing through a glass prism separates into colors. | 2
- diagonal | 对角线 | A straight line joining two non-adjacent corners of a shape | 连接图形中不相邻两个顶点的线段。 | The diagonal of a square splits it into two right triangles. | 2
@topic 1.3 | Size and Units | 大小与单位 | Measure and convert quantities
- perimeter | 周长（多边形） | The total distance around the edge of a flat shape | 平面图形边界一周的总长度。 | The perimeter of a 3 m by 5 m garden is 16 m. | 1
  vs: area
- area | 面积 | The amount of surface a flat shape covers, measured in square units | 平面图形所占的大小，单位是平方单位（如 m²）。 | The area under a force–time graph equals the impulse. | 1
  mis: Area and perimeter are the same thing — area measures surface (m²), perimeter measures length around (m).
  vs: perimeter; volume
- surface area | 表面积 | The total area of all the outer surfaces of a 3-D object | 立体所有外表面面积之和。 | Small cells have a large surface area relative to their volume. | 1
- volume | 体积 | The amount of space a 3-D object or substance takes up | 物体或物质所占空间的大小，单位是立方单位或升。 | The volume of gas doubles when its temperature in kelvins doubles at constant pressure. | 1
  vs: area
- cross-sectional area | 横截面积 | The area of the flat surface you get when you cut straight across an object | 垂直切开物体所得截面的面积。 | Water flows faster where the pipe's cross-sectional area is smaller. | 2
- dimension | 维度；尺寸 | A measurement such as length, width or height; also the number of directions an object extends in | 长、宽、高等尺寸；也指空间的维数（一维、二维、三维）。 | Check the dimensions of your answer: area should be in square meters. | 2
- length | 长度 | How long something is from one end to the other | 物体从一端到另一端的距离。 | The length of the pendulum determines its period. | 1
- width | 宽度 | How wide something is from side to side | 物体从一侧到另一侧的距离。 | The rectangle has a width of 4 cm. | 1
- height | 高度 | How tall something is, or the vertical distance above a reference level | 物体的高，或相对参考面的竖直距离。 | Gravitational potential energy depends on height. | 1
- unit | 单位 | A standard quantity used to measure something, such as meters or seconds | 计量所用的标准量，如米、秒、千克。答案通常要写单位。 | Always include units, such as m/s, in your final answer. | 1
- unit conversion | 单位换算 | Changing a measurement from one unit to another | 把一个量从一种单位换成另一种单位，如 km/h → m/s。 | Convert 72 km/h to 20 m/s before using the formula. | 2
- scale factor | 比例因子 | The number by which all lengths are multiplied when a shape is enlarged or reduced | 图形放大或缩小时所有长度乘的倍数；面积按平方变化。 | With a scale factor of 2, the area becomes 4 times larger. | 2
- significant figures | 有效数字 | The digits in a measurement that carry meaning about its precision | 测量值中有意义的数字位数，反映精确程度。 | Report the answer to three significant figures: 9.81 m/s². | 2
- scientific notation | 科学记数法 | Writing a number as a value between 1 and 10 times a power of 10 | 把数写成 a × 10ⁿ（1 ≤ a < 10）的形式。 | Avogadro's number is 6.02 × 10²³ in scientific notation. | 1
- order of magnitude | 数量级 | The power of ten closest to a number, used to compare rough sizes | 数值最接近的 10 的幂，用来粗略比较大小。 | The two masses differ by three orders of magnitude (1,000 times). | 2

@unit 2 | Numbers and Algebra | 数与代数
@topic 2.1 | Number Words | 数的用语 | Read number vocabulary in questions
- fraction | 分数 | A number written as one integer over another, showing parts of a whole | 表示整体的一部分的数，如 3/4。 | About one fraction of the energy, 1/10, passes to the next trophic level. | 1
- decimal | 小数 | A number written with a decimal point, such as 0.75 | 用小数点表示的数。 | Write 3/8 as the decimal 0.375. | 1
- percent | 百分比 | A number out of 100, written with % | 以 100 为分母的比率，如 25% = 0.25。 | The unemployment rate is 4 percent. | 1
- percent change | 百分比变化 | The change in a value divided by its original value, times 100 | (新值 − 原值) ÷ 原值 × 100%。 | Price rose from $10 to $12, a percent change of 20%. | 2
  mis: Percent change uses the new value as the denominator — divide by the ORIGINAL value.
- ratio | 比；比率 | A comparison of two quantities by division, written a:b or a/b | 两个量相除的比较，如 3:2。 | The ratio of red to white flowers is 3:1. | 1
  vs: rate
- proportion | 比例 | An equation stating that two ratios are equal; also a part of a whole | 两个比相等的式子；也可指“所占比例”。 | The proportion of students who passed was 0.8. | 2
- rate | 速率；比率 | A ratio comparing quantities with different units, such as meters per second | 带不同单位的两个量之比，如米/秒、元/小时；也指变化的快慢。 | The reaction rate doubled when the temperature increased. | 1
  vs: ratio
- exponent | 指数 | The small raised number showing how many times a base is multiplied by itself | 写在右上角的数，表示底数自乘的次数，如 2³ 中的 3。 | In 10⁶, the exponent is 6. | 1
- product | 积 | The result of multiplying numbers | 相乘的结果。注意化学中 product 指“生成物”。 | The product of mass and velocity is momentum. | 1
- prime number | 质数（素数） | A whole number greater than 1 whose only factors are 1 and itself | 只能被 1 和它本身整除的大于 1 的整数。 | 2, 3, 5 and 7 are prime numbers. | 1
- multiple | 倍数 | The result of multiplying a number by a whole number | 一个数乘以整数得到的数。 | 12 is a multiple of 3 and of 4. | 1
- average | 平均数 | The sum of values divided by how many values there are | 一组数据之和除以数据个数（即 mean）。 | The average speed is total distance divided by total time. | 1
- maximum | 最大值 | The greatest value | 最大的值。 | The ball reaches its maximum height when its vertical velocity is zero. | 1
  vs: minimum
- minimum | 最小值 | The least value | 最小的值。 | Average total cost is at its minimum where marginal cost crosses it. | 1
  vs: maximum
- positive | 正的 | Greater than zero; also pointing in the chosen positive direction | 大于零的；也指沿规定正方向的。 | A positive slope means the graph rises from left to right. | 1
- negative | 负的 | Less than zero; also pointing opposite the positive direction | 小于零的；也指与正方向相反的。 | A negative acceleration points opposite the positive direction. | 1
@topic 2.2 | Equations and Relationships | 方程与关系 | Describe how quantities relate
- equation | 方程；等式 | A statement that two expressions are equal | 含等号、表示两个式子相等的数学语句。 | Use the equation F = ma to find the acceleration. | 1
  vs: expression
- expression | 表达式 | A combination of numbers, variables and operations without an equals sign | 由数、变量和运算组成、没有等号的式子。 | Simplify the expression 3x + 2x to 5x. | 1
  vs: equation
- formula | 公式 | An equation that shows a rule relating quantities | 表示量之间固定关系的式子，如 A = πr²。 | The formula for kinetic energy is KE = ½mv². | 1
- solve | 求解 | To find the value(s) that make an equation true | 求出使方程成立的值。 | Solve for t in d = ½at². | 1
- substitute into | 代入 | To replace a variable with a known value | 把已知值代替公式中的变量。 | Substitute m = 2 kg into the equation. | 1
- linear relationship | 线性关系 | A relationship that forms a straight line when graphed; one quantity changes at a constant rate with the other | 图像为直线的关系，一个量随另一个量以恒定速率变化。 | Hooke's law shows a linear relationship between force and stretch. | 2
  vs: nonlinear relationship
- nonlinear relationship | 非线性关系 | A relationship whose graph is not a straight line | 图像不是直线的关系，如二次、指数关系。 | Kinetic energy has a nonlinear (quadratic) relationship with speed. | 2
  vs: linear relationship
- quadratic | 二次的 | Involving a squared term, such as x² | 含平方项的（最高次为 2）。 | Distance under constant acceleration is quadratic in time. | 2
- inverse relationship | 反向关系 | When one quantity increases, the other decreases | 一个量增大时另一个量减小（不一定是严格反比）。 | Price and quantity demanded have an inverse relationship. | 2
  vs: direct relationship
- direct relationship | 正向关系 | When one quantity increases, the other also increases | 一个量增大时另一个量也增大。 | Temperature and gas pressure have a direct relationship at constant volume. | 2
  vs: inverse relationship
- squared | 平方的 | Multiplied by itself; raised to the power 2 | 自乘一次，即二次方。 | Gravitational force depends on distance squared. | 1
- cubed | 立方的 | Raised to the power 3 | 三次方。 | Volume of a cube equals side length cubed. | 1

@unit 3 | Graphs and Data | 图表与数据
@topic 3.1 | Reading Graphs | 读图 | Describe what a graph shows
- axis | 坐标轴 | A reference line on a graph along which values are measured (plural: axes) | 图表中用来标记数值的参考线，复数为 axes。 | Label each axis with the quantity and its units. | 1
- x-axis | x 轴（横轴） | The horizontal axis of a graph | 水平方向的坐标轴，通常表示自变量。 | Time is plotted on the x-axis. | 1
- y-axis | y 轴（纵轴） | The vertical axis of a graph | 竖直方向的坐标轴，通常表示因变量。 | Velocity is plotted on the y-axis. | 1
- horizontal | 水平的 | Going straight across, parallel to the ground or x-axis | 与地面或 x 轴平行的方向。 | A horizontal line on a position–time graph means the object is at rest. | 1
  vs: vertical
- vertical | 竖直的；垂直的 | Going straight up and down, parallel to the y-axis | 与地面垂直、上下方向的。 | The vertical component of velocity changes due to gravity. | 1
  vs: horizontal
- data point | 数据点 | A single value or pair of values plotted on a graph | 图上标出的一个数据。 | One data point lies far from the trend — it may be an outlier. | 1
- line graph | 折线图 | A graph that connects data points with lines to show change | 用线连接数据点、显示变化趋势的图。 | A line graph shows how GDP changed from 2000 to 2020. | 1
- bar graph | 柱状图 | A graph that uses bars to compare amounts across categories | 用柱子高度比较各类别数量的图。 | A bar graph compares the populations of four species. | 1
- histogram | 直方图 | A graph showing how many data values fall in each interval | 显示数据落在各区间内个数的图，柱子之间没有间隔。 | The histogram of test scores is skewed to the right. | 2
  vs: bar graph
- pie chart | 饼图 | A circle divided into sectors showing parts of a whole | 用扇形表示各部分占整体比例的圆形图。 | A pie chart shows the share of each energy source. | 1
- data table | 数据表 | Rows and columns that organize measured values | 用行和列整理测量数据的表格。 | Record each trial's result in the data table. | 1
- legend | 图例 | The key that explains what colors or symbols on a graph mean | 说明图中颜色或符号含义的标注。 | Use the legend to tell which line is the control group. | 1
- curve | 曲线 | A line on a graph that is not straight | 图上不是直线的线。 | The curve levels off at the carrying capacity. | 1
- steep | 陡峭的 | Having a large slope; changing quickly | 斜率大的，变化快的。 | A steeper position–time graph means a faster object. | 1
- level off | 趋于平稳 | To stop rising or falling and become flat | 停止上升或下降，变得平坦。 | The population graph levels off near 500. | 1
- peak | 峰值；顶点 | The highest point on a graph | 图像的最高点。 | Enzyme activity reaches a peak at 37°C. | 1
- fluctuate | 波动 | To rise and fall irregularly | 上下不规则地变化。 | Stock prices fluctuate from day to day. | 2
- correlate | 相关 | To have a relationship in which two variables change together | 两个变量一起变化、存在关联。 | Study time correlates with test scores, but that does not prove causation. | 2
- interpolate | 内插 | To estimate a value between known data points | 在已知数据点之间估计数值。 | Interpolate from the graph to estimate the value at t = 2.5 s. | 2
  vs: extrapolate
- extrapolate | 外推 | To estimate a value beyond the range of known data | 在已知数据范围之外进行估计，风险较大。 | Extrapolating the trend to 2050 assumes the pattern continues. | 2
  vs: interpolate

@unit 4 | Lab and Experiment | 实验与探究
@topic 4.1 | Designing Investigations | 实验设计 | Use investigation vocabulary
- measurement | 测量；测量值 | A value found by measuring, including a number and a unit | 通过测量得到的值，包括数值和单位。 | Each measurement was repeated three times. | 1
- trial | 试验（一次） | One run of an experiment | 实验的一次重复操作。 | The average of five trials is more reliable than one trial. | 1
- control group | 对照组 | The group that does not receive the treatment, used for comparison | 不接受处理、用于比较的组。 | The control group received a placebo. | 1
  vs: experimental group
- experimental group | 实验组 | The group that receives the treatment being tested | 接受所测试处理的组。 | The experimental group received the new fertilizer. | 1
  vs: control group
- controlled variable | 控制变量 | A factor kept the same in all groups so it does not affect results | 在各组中保持不变、避免影响结果的因素。 | Water volume is a controlled variable in the plant experiment. | 2
- procedure | 实验步骤 | The step-by-step method used in an investigation | 实验的具体操作步骤。 | Describe a procedure to measure the spring constant. | 1
- apparatus | 实验装置 | The equipment used in an experiment | 实验中使用的仪器和装置。 | Draw a diagram of the apparatus you would use. | 2
- observation | 观察；观察结果 | Information gathered using the senses or instruments | 用感官或仪器获得的信息。 | The observation that the solution turned blue indicates a reaction. | 1
- qualitative | 定性的 | Described with words or qualities, not numbers | 用文字描述性质，而不是数值。 | "The solution became cloudy" is a qualitative observation. | 2
  vs: quantitative
- quantitative | 定量的 | Described with numbers and measurements | 用数值和测量结果描述。 | "The mass increased by 2.4 g" is a quantitative observation. | 2
  vs: qualitative
- conclusion | 结论 | A statement of what the results show, supported by data | 根据数据得出的结果说明。 | The conclusion should refer to the hypothesis and the evidence. | 1
- accuracy | 准确度 | How close a measurement is to the true or accepted value | 测量值与真实值（公认值）接近的程度。 | The student's value of g = 9.79 m/s² shows high accuracy. | 2
  mis: Accurate and precise mean the same — accurate is close to the true value; precise is close to each other.
  vs: precision
- precision | 精密度 | How close repeated measurements are to each other | 多次测量结果彼此接近的程度。 | Five readings within 0.01 s show high precision. | 2
  vs: accuracy
- uncertainty | 不确定度 | The range within which the true value is expected to lie | 真实值可能所在的范围，如 ±0.1 cm。 | The length is 12.4 ± 0.1 cm, so the uncertainty is 0.1 cm. | 2
- percent error | 百分误差 | The difference between a measured and accepted value, divided by the accepted value, times 100 | |测量值 − 公认值| ÷ 公认值 × 100%。 | A measured g of 9.5 m/s² has a percent error of about 3%. | 2
- systematic error | 系统误差 | An error that shifts all measurements in the same direction | 使所有测量结果朝同一方向偏离的误差，如仪器未校零。 | A scale that always reads 0.5 g too high causes systematic error. | 3
  vs: random error
- random error | 随机误差 | Unpredictable variation in measurements, reduced by averaging trials | 不可预测的测量波动，可通过多次测量取平均减小。 | Reaction-time differences in using a stopwatch cause random error. | 3
  vs: systematic error
@topic 4.2 | Lab Equipment | 实验仪器 | Name common lab equipment
- beaker | 烧杯 | A cylindrical glass container used to hold and mix liquids | 用来盛放和混合液体的圆柱形玻璃容器。 | Pour 50 mL of water into a beaker. | 1
- graduated cylinder | 量筒 | A tall cylinder with markings used to measure liquid volume accurately | 有刻度、用于较准确测量液体体积的细长容器。 | Read the graduated cylinder at the bottom of the meniscus. | 1
- balance | 天平 | An instrument used to measure mass | 测量质量的仪器。 | Use an electronic balance to find the mass of the sample. | 1
- thermometer | 温度计 | An instrument used to measure temperature | 测量温度的仪器。 | Record the thermometer reading every 30 seconds. | 1
- stopwatch | 秒表 | A device used to measure time intervals | 测量时间间隔的仪器。 | Time ten swings with a stopwatch and divide by ten. | 1
- test tube | 试管 | A small glass tube used to hold samples | 盛放少量样品的小玻璃管。 | Add three drops of indicator to each test tube. | 1

@unit 5 | Basic Physical Science | 基础物理化学
@topic 5.1 | Matter | 物质 | Use basic matter vocabulary
- matter | 物质 | Anything that has mass and takes up space | 有质量并占据空间的任何东西。 | All matter is made of atoms. | 1
- atom | 原子 | The smallest unit of an element that keeps its chemical properties | 保持元素化学性质的最小单位。 | A carbon atom has six protons. | 1
- molecule | 分子 | Two or more atoms bonded together | 两个或多个原子通过化学键结合而成的粒子。 | A water molecule contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. | 1
- element | 元素 | A pure substance made of only one kind of atom | 只由一种原子组成的纯净物。 | Oxygen is an element listed on the periodic table. | 1
  vs: compound
- compound | 化合物 | A substance made of two or more elements chemically bonded | 由两种或以上元素化学结合而成的物质。 | Water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen. | 1
  vs: element; mixture
- mixture | 混合物 | Two or more substances combined physically, not chemically | 两种或以上物质物理混合，没有发生化学结合。 | Air is a mixture of gases. | 1
  vs: compound
- solution | 溶液 | A uniform mixture in which one substance is dissolved in another | 一种物质均匀溶解在另一种物质中形成的混合物。 | Salt water is a solution. | 1
- solute | 溶质 | The substance that is dissolved in a solution | 被溶解的物质。 | In salt water, salt is the solute. | 1
  vs: solvent
- solvent | 溶剂 | The substance that does the dissolving | 起溶解作用的物质，通常是量较多的那种。 | Water is called the universal solvent. | 1
  vs: solute
- concentration | 浓度 | The amount of a substance in a given volume | 单位体积中某物质的量。 | Oxygen diffuses from high concentration to low concentration. | 1
- solid | 固体 | A state of matter with a fixed shape and volume | 有固定形状和体积的物质状态。 | Ice is the solid form of water. | 1
- liquid | 液体 | A state of matter with a fixed volume but no fixed shape | 有固定体积但没有固定形状的物质状态。 | A liquid takes the shape of its container. | 1
- gas | 气体 | A state of matter with no fixed shape or volume | 没有固定形状和体积的物质状态。 | A gas expands to fill its container. | 1
- particle | 粒子 | A tiny piece of matter, such as an atom, ion or molecule | 构成物质的微小单位，如原子、离子、分子。 | Particles move faster at higher temperatures. | 1
- ion | 离子 | An atom or group of atoms with an electric charge | 带电的原子或原子团。 | Na⁺ is a positive ion. | 1
@topic 5.2 | Energy and Change | 能量与变化 | Describe energy and changes of state
- energy | 能量 | The ability to do work or cause change, measured in joules | 做功或引起变化的能力，单位焦耳（J）。 | Energy is conserved in a closed system. | 1
- temperature | 温度 | A measure of the average kinetic energy of particles | 物体冷热程度，反映粒子平均动能。 | Temperature must be in kelvins in the ideal gas law. | 1
  vs: heat
- heat | 热量 | Energy transferred because of a temperature difference | 由于温度差而传递的能量。 | Heat flows from the hot object to the cold one. | 2
  mis: Heat and temperature are the same — heat is energy transferred; temperature measures how hot something is.
  vs: temperature
- melting point | 熔点 | The temperature at which a solid turns into a liquid | 固体变成液体的温度。 | Ice has a melting point of 0°C. | 1
- boiling point | 沸点 | The temperature at which a liquid turns into a gas throughout | 液体沸腾变成气体的温度。 | Water's boiling point is lower at high altitude. | 1
- evaporation | 蒸发 | The change of a liquid into a gas at its surface | 液体表面变成气体的过程。 | Evaporation of sweat cools the body. | 1
  vs: condensation
- condensation | 凝结 | The change of a gas into a liquid | 气体变成液体的过程。 | Condensation forms on a cold glass. | 1
  vs: evaporation
- chemical reaction | 化学反应 | A process in which substances change into new substances | 物质变成新物质的过程。 | Bubbles of gas are evidence of a chemical reaction. | 1
- reactant | 反应物 | A starting substance in a chemical reaction | 化学反应开始时的物质，写在箭头左边。 | In photosynthesis, CO₂ and water are reactants. | 1
  vs: product (chemistry)
- product (chemistry) | 生成物 | A substance formed in a chemical reaction | 化学反应生成的物质，写在箭头右边。 | Oxygen is a product of photosynthesis. | 1
  vs: reactant
- speed | 速率 | How fast something moves; distance per unit time | 物体运动的快慢，单位时间内的路程。 | The car's speed is 20 m/s. | 1
- electric charge | 电荷 | A property of matter that causes electric forces; positive or negative | 物质产生电力作用的性质，分正负。 | Like charges repel and opposite charges attract. | 1

@unit 6 | Basic Life and Earth Science | 基础生命与地球科学
@topic 6.1 | Living Things | 生物 | Use basic biology vocabulary
- organism | 生物（个体） | An individual living thing | 一个有生命的个体。 | Bacteria are single-celled organisms. | 1
- cell | 细胞 | The basic unit of structure and function in living things | 生物体结构和功能的基本单位。 | All organisms are made of one or more cells. | 1
- tissue | 组织 | A group of similar cells working together | 由相似细胞组成、共同完成功能的结构。 | Muscle tissue contracts to produce movement. | 1
- organ | 器官 | A structure made of tissues that performs a specific function | 由多种组织构成、执行特定功能的结构。 | The heart is an organ that pumps blood. | 1
- species | 物种 | A group of organisms that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring | 能相互交配并产生可育后代的一群生物。 | Lions and tigers are different species. | 1
- bacteria | 细菌 | Single-celled organisms without a nucleus (singular: bacterium) | 没有细胞核的单细胞生物，单数为 bacterium。 | Some bacteria cause disease, but many are helpful. | 1
  vs: virus
- virus | 病毒 | A tiny non-cellular particle that can reproduce only inside a host cell | 非细胞结构，只能在宿主细胞内复制的微小颗粒。 | Antibiotics do not work against a virus. | 1
  mis: Antibiotics kill viruses — they act on bacteria, not viruses.
  vs: bacteria
- nutrient | 营养物质 | A substance an organism needs to live and grow | 生物生存和生长所需的物质。 | Plants absorb nutrients such as nitrogen from the soil. | 1
- protein | 蛋白质 | A large molecule made of amino acids that does much of the work in cells | 由氨基酸组成的大分子，承担细胞中的大部分功能。 | Enzymes are proteins. | 1
- DNA | 脱氧核糖核酸（DNA） | The molecule that carries genetic information | 携带遗传信息的分子。 | DNA is copied before a cell divides. | 1
- gene | 基因 | A section of DNA that codes for a trait or protein | DNA 上决定性状或编码蛋白质的片段。 | A gene for eye color has several alleles. | 1
- ecosystem | 生态系统 | All the living things in an area plus their physical environment | 一个区域内所有生物与其非生物环境的总和。 | A pond ecosystem includes fish, plants, water and sunlight. | 1
@topic 6.2 | Earth and Environment | 地球与环境 | Use basic environmental vocabulary
- atmosphere | 大气层 | The layer of gases surrounding Earth | 包围地球的气体层。 | Carbon dioxide in the atmosphere traps heat. | 1
- climate | 气候 | The long-term pattern of weather in a region | 一个地区长期的天气模式。 | Climate change affects where species can live. | 1
  vs: weather
- weather | 天气 | The short-term condition of the atmosphere | 短期的大气状况。 | The weather today is rainy, but the climate is dry. | 1
  vs: climate
- resource | 资源 | Something useful that people or organisms use, such as water or land | 人或生物可利用的东西，如水、土地、矿产。 | Water is a scarce resource in deserts. | 1
- pollution | 污染 | Harmful substances added to the environment | 向环境中排放的有害物质。 | Air pollution from factories is a negative externality. | 1
- renewable resource | 可再生资源 | A resource that can be replaced naturally over a short time | 能在短时间内自然恢复的资源，如太阳能、风能。 | Solar power is a renewable resource. | 1
  vs: nonrenewable resource
- nonrenewable resource | 不可再生资源 | A resource that cannot be replaced within a human lifetime | 在人类时间尺度内无法再生的资源，如煤、石油。 | Oil is a nonrenewable resource. | 1
  vs: renewable resource

# ---------------- Practice items (original) ----------------
@q fnd-u1-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | MCQ | 1 | 1 | radius; diameter
? A circular track has a diameter of 100 m. What is its radius?
= 100 m :: That is the diameter.
=* 50 m :: Correct — the radius is half the diameter.
= 200 m :: That would be twice the diameter.
= 314 m :: That is the circumference.
! The radius is the distance from the center to the edge, half of the diameter.
!zh 半径是圆心到圆周的距离，是直径的一半。
@q fnd-u1-002 | APPLY | APPLICATION | SCENARIO | 1 | 1 | area; perimeter
? A farmer wants to know how much fencing to buy for a rectangular field. Which quantity should she calculate?
= Area :: Area tells how much ground the field covers, not the fence length.
=* Perimeter :: Correct — fencing goes around the edge.
= Volume :: The field is flat; volume does not apply.
= Diameter :: Diameter applies to circles.
! Fencing follows the boundary, so the perimeter gives the length needed.
!zh 篱笆沿边界围一圈，所以要计算周长。
@q fnd-u4-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 4 | 2 | accuracy; precision
? Four students measure g and get 9.20, 9.21, 9.20 and 9.21 m/s². The accepted value is 9.81 m/s². Their results are
= accurate and precise. :: They are far from 9.81, so they are not accurate.
=* precise but not accurate. :: Correct — the values agree with each other but are far from the true value.
= accurate but not precise. :: The values are very close together, so they are precise.
= neither accurate nor precise. :: They are precise.
! Precision = agreement among repeated measurements; accuracy = closeness to the true value.
!zh 精密度看测量值彼此是否接近；准确度看是否接近真实值。
@q fnd-u5-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | MCQ | 5 | 1 | solute; solvent
? When sugar dissolves in tea, the sugar is the
=* solute. :: Correct — sugar is the substance being dissolved.
= solvent. :: The tea (water) is the solvent.
= compound. :: Sugar is a compound, but in this context its role is the solute.
= precipitate. :: A precipitate is a solid that forms out of a solution.
! The solute dissolves; the solvent does the dissolving.
!zh 溶质被溶解，溶剂起溶解作用。

@pair radius-vs-diameter | radius | diameter
! The radius goes from the center to the edge; the diameter goes all the way across through the center. d = 2r.
!zh 半径从圆心到圆周；直径穿过圆心横跨整个圆。d = 2r。
d: Goes through center? :: Starts at the center :: Passes through the center
d: Formula link :: r = d ÷ 2 :: d = 2r
s: A :: The length of a clock's hand from the center to the tip. :: Center to edge.
s: B :: The width of a coin measured straight across. :: All the way across.
s: A :: Used directly in A = πr². :: Area formula uses the radius.
s: B :: A pizza described as "12 inches" across. :: Measured across = diameter.
@pair accuracy-vs-precision | accuracy | precision
! Accuracy is how close a result is to the true value; precision is how close repeated results are to each other.
!zh 准确度：接近真实值的程度；精密度：多次结果彼此接近的程度。
d: Compared with :: The true / accepted value :: Other measurements
d: Improved by :: Calibrating equipment, removing systematic error :: Better instruments, careful technique
s: A :: The average measured value is 9.80 m/s², very close to the accepted 9.81. :: Close to the true value.
s: B :: All ten readings are within 0.01 g of each other. :: Close to each other.
s: B :: Darts land in a tight group in the corner of the board. :: Clustered but off target.
s: A :: A scale is recalibrated so it reads the true mass. :: Fixing systematic error improves accuracy.
@pair area-vs-perimeter | area | perimeter
! Area is the space inside a flat shape (square units); perimeter is the distance around its edge (length units).
!zh 面积是图形内部的大小（平方单位）；周长是边界的长度（长度单位）。
s: A :: How much carpet is needed to cover a floor. :: Covering a surface = area.
s: B :: How much ribbon is needed to go around a picture frame. :: Going around = perimeter.
s: A :: Measured in m². :: Square units = area.
s: B :: Measured in m. :: Length units = perimeter.
@pair heat-vs-temperature | heat | temperature
! Temperature measures how hot something is (average particle energy); heat is energy that moves because of a temperature difference.
!zh 温度表示冷热程度（粒子平均动能）；热量是因温度差而转移的能量。
s: B :: A thermometer reads 25°C. :: A measurement of hotness.
s: A :: Energy flows from hot coffee into the cold mug. :: Energy transfer = heat.
s: A :: Measured in joules. :: Heat is energy.
s: B :: Measured in kelvins or degrees Celsius. :: Temperature units.
